	abstract = {As grid-forming (GFM) inverters replace synchronous machines, classical transient stability tools fall short in capturing their nonlinear dynamics—particularly under current-limited conditions. This paper introduces a two-bus framework that enables analytical characterization of transient stability in multi-inverter systems. By aggregating parallel-connected GFM inverters into an equivalent source, we derive fault-induced angle dynamics and identify critical clearing conditions. A key contribution is the formulation of an interaction energy term that quantifies power exchange between inverters, showing how electrical distance and parameter mismatches—in inertia, damping, and power reference—affect angle divergence and stability margins. The newly developed analytical expressions reveal that the interaction energy increases with the severity of the mismatch and is mitigated by a greater electrical distance between the inverters. Numerical simulations in PLECS validate the proposed analytical formulation.},
