	doi = {10.1109/ECCE58356.2025.11260192},
	abstract = {With the increasing deployment of inverter-based resources (IBRs), grid-forming (GFM) inverters have attracted significant attention for their ability to maintain grid stability without relying on conventional synchronous generators. However, the strict overcurrent limitations of GFM inverters pose major challenges for achieving stable inverter-dominated operation. While various current-limiting and stability enhancement methods have been proposed, most rely on the single-machine-infinite-bus (SMIB) assumption, which becomes invalid in weak-grid or stand-alone microgrid scenarios, rendering conventional tools such as power-angle analysis ineffective. To address this limitation, this paper presents a novel stability analysis framework for GFM-inverter-dominated networks based on a quasi-steady-state modeling approach that avoids the need for a stiff voltage reference. A new stability index is introduced, derived from the fault-bus voltage phasor dynamics, to assess post-fault convergence and estimate the critical clearing time (CCT). The proposed method is validated through detailed EMT simulations and is shown to offer more accurate and conservative CCT estimates than traditional power-angle-based indices. The framework is applicable and extensible to different GFM control strategies and current-limiting methods, making it suitable for interoperability studies and large-scale system analysis.},
